\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{One permutation-group link has four isotypic states but one charged state}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take one oriented edge with distinct source and target and
#{G=S_3}. Then #{\mathcal H_{\mathrm{link}}=L^2(S_3)} has
complex dimension six. Order the gauge factors as
#{K=S_3^{\mathrm{target}}\times S_3^{\mathrm{source}}}; their
[function action](fdip-0089) is
##{(R_{(h_t,h_s)}f)(U)=f(h_t^{-1}Uh_s).}
Let #{V=\{(z_1,z_2,z_3)\in\mathbb C^3:z_1+z_2+z_3=0\}}
carry the standard unitary permutation representation #{\rho}.
Use the charge datum
##{R=V_{\mathrm{target}}\otimes V_{\mathrm{source}}^*.}
It has dimension four.}

\p{The standard representation is irreducible: a three-cycle has
two distinct eigenlines on #{V}, with eigenvalues
#{e^{2\pi i/3}} and #{e^{-2\pi i/3}}, and a transposition
exchanges them. Thus neither line is invariant under #{S_3}.
The external tensor product #{R} is also irreducible under #{K}.
To see this, expand an endomorphism in a basis of
#{\operatorname{End}(V^*)}. Commutation with the first factor
and Schur's lemma make it #{I\otimes B}; commutation with the
second factor makes #{B} scalar. Any invariant subspace has a
commuting orthogonal projector, so it is zero or all of #{R}.}

\p{The inverse matrix-coefficient map is
##{\begin{aligned}
\Psi:R&\longrightarrow L^2(S_3),\\
\Psi(v\otimes\lambda)(U)
 &=\sqrt2\,\lambda(\rho(U^{-1})v).
\end{aligned}}
[Schur orthogonality](fdip-007C) makes this map isometric for
normalized counting measure. Its order of duals is checked directly:
##{R_{(h_t,h_s)}\Psi(v\otimes\lambda)
 =\Psi(\rho(h_t)v\otimes\rho^*(h_s)\lambda).}
Thus its four-dimensional image is a copy of the specified charge
representation, with the target and source conventions as stated.}

\p{The [finite Peter–Weyl decomposition](fdip-007C) gives the
bilateral decomposition
##{L^2(S_3)\cong
 (\mathbf1\otimes\mathbf1)
 \oplus(\operatorname{sgn}\otimes\operatorname{sgn})
 \oplus(V\otimes V^*).}
Indeed, the trivial, sign and standard representations are
inequivalent, and their squared dimensions sum to
#{1+1+4=6=|S_3|}, so their orthogonal coefficient spaces exhaust
#{L^2(S_3)}. Hence the standard bilateral type has multiplicity
one. By the [charged-space theorem](fdip-008B),
##{\dim\mathcal H_R=4,\qquad
 \dim\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}(R)
 =\dim\operatorname{Hom}_K(R,L^2(S_3))=1.}
The four-dimensional isotypic component is therefore not the
charged physical space. Isotypic projectors separate representation
types; invariant charge tensors supply the Gauss constraint.}
